A||||3||Richard Parnell||Tranny Differences?||||5||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||06-12-2000||06:17 PM||||As I've mentioned before I'm in the process of installing a V8 in my 87 SC. My question, is there a difference between the Isuzu 5 speed for a 4 cylinder and one from a 6 cylinder?  I can't see any reason why there would be, but some comments I've heard lately seem to indicate there is. <P>Also, when I buy a slave or master for the trans I'm asked if it's for a 5 speed or 4 speed, are there diferences in the sizes?||||reg||5
Z||000001||fieroshop||06-12-2000||06:38 PM||||There are two different 5 speeds. The Getrag and the Izuzu. Also the 4 speed is a different tranny. The are all interchangesble to the engines but the brackets for the cables and the slave cylinder is different fot each model trans.<p>[This message has been edited by fieroshop (edited 06-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Patrick's Dad||06-12-2000||11:18 PM||||I've heard (mostly from this forum) that the Isuzu Xaxles had issues witht he mounting of the slave cylinder. Having had an '85 2m4 with the I5 and two clutch replacements in three years, I tend to say that they weren't perfect....<P>Now, my '88 Formula has the Getrag 5sp (282), and I've replaced one clutch in nine years (Thanks, mostly to the throwout bearing....). It is a cleaner shifter (although the I5 wasn't bad) with a better feel. It's not as smooth as my friend's Miata, but I't feels like I can beat up on it a bit without worry.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Richard Parnell||06-13-2000||06:07 PM||||Patrick's Dad-  What were the issues with the mounting of the slave on the Isuzu tranny that you recall? My clutch will not disengage presently and all it needs is about another 1/4 inch to work.  The master and slave have been endlessly bled and have no leaks.  It would be nice to still drive my car while I'm gathering all the parts for the V8 conversion.||||reg||5
